    "type": "market-brief",
    "title": "Seoul’s Euljiro: read the signs before finding your café",
    "deck": "An afternoon among printing shops, tools, lighting and small restaurants. Discover the neighbourhood through the things it helps make.",
    "bodyMarkdown": "![Printing shop frontage in Euljiro, Seoul](/assets/editorial-2026-09/seoul-euljiro-print-shop.jpg \"Euljiro printing alley · Aaaatu / Wikimedia Commons / CC BY-SA 4.0 · 20 October 2021\")\n\nWhen meeting someone in Euljiro, it is easy to consult your phone before looking at a shop sign. Which floor is the café on? Does its entrance face the main road or a lane? Once you arrive, the businesses you passed become background. Try reversing that sequence: before entering the café, read the trades named on the signs.\n\nJung-gu’s district office describes Euljiro’s urban industries as including tools, lighting, sewing machines, tiles and ceramics, engraving, furniture, printing and machinery. A map for eating and drinking overlaps with a map for making and repairing things. This suggested walk explores the printing lanes and the surroundings of Sewoon Sangga before continuing towards Cheonggyecheon. It is an editorial itinerary based on published material, not a report of a recent visit. [Jung-gu’s guide to Euljiro’s specialised streets](https://www.junggu.seoul.kr/content.do?cmsid=14207)\n\n## Notice the work behind the street\n\nUse the area around Euljiro 3-ga Station as a starting point, without heading straight for a restaurant queue. Locate public lanes towards Chungmuro and Inhyeon-dong on a current map. Rather than completing every street, try recognising two or three trades that catch your attention.\n\nPrinting itself involves several stages: choosing paper, printing, cutting and binding it into a book or package. A sign may describe one stage rather than the finished object. Note an unfamiliar term and look it up during a break. There is no need to interrupt someone at work for an explanation.\n\nThe Seoul Museum of History’s record of Inhyeon-dong explains that printing businesses moved into the area after redevelopment began in Janggyo-dong in 1983. It is a useful reminder that the lanes took shape as workplaces moved within the city. That history does not establish that the same businesses or buildings survive today. [Seoul History Archive: Inhyeon-dong](https://museum.seoul.go.kr/archive/archiveNew/NR_archiveList.do?ctgryId=CTGRY333&subCtgryId=CTGRY336&type=A)\n\nWatch the space people need to move through before composing a photograph. Goods and entrances outside a workshop may be part of a working day, not a display. Step aside for deliveries, and ask before entering an interior. Signs visible from the street already offer plenty to discover.\n\n![A print operator checking work inside an Euljiro workshop](/assets/editorial-2026-09/seoul-euljiro-quality-control.jpg \"Print work and quality control · Aaaatu / Wikimedia Commons / CC BY-SA 4.0 · 20 October 2021\")\n\n## Connect what you have seen over coffee\n\nLeave room for lunch or coffee along the way. A seat, a menu you want and a location near your next stop can be more useful criteria than finding a particular fashionable interior. Avoiding a plan dependent on one shop’s opening hours or queue gives you more freedom to linger.\n\nWhile resting, work backwards from an object on the table. A menu, business card or takeaway box needs paper, printing, cutting and finishing as well as design. When a word from a sign connects with that process, the street becomes easier to remember. You are considering what the neighbourhood produces as well as what it shows visitors.\n\nNor do you need to decide that established trades represent the authentic Euljiro while new cafés are merely decoration. Their customers also contribute to the street today. Notice how closely different uses sit beside each other. Remembering that people may visit different floors of a building for different reasons can make even the search for an entrance more considerate.\n\nNext to your note about a café you liked, write down one trade you learned about. Recording a recommendation and a discovery gives you a reason to choose another lane next time.\n\n![Another printing workshop in Euljiro, Seoul](/assets/editorial-2026-09/seoul-euljiro-another-printer.jpg \"Euljiro printing workshop · Aaaatu / Wikimedia Commons / CC BY-SA 4.0 · 20 October 2021\")\n\n## Widen the view around Sewoon Sangga\n\nAround Sewoon Sangga, look at the large buildings and the streets together. A Seoul city publication describes the shopping-building group as extending from Jongno towards Toegye-ro. Rather than attempting the entire stretch, choose a section near Cheonggyecheon. Step back from individual shops to consider the buildings’ length, their relationship with the road and the directions people enter from. [Seoul’s introduction to Sewoon Sangga](https://mediahub.seoul.go.kr/archives/1133222)\n\nRooftops and connecting walkways mentioned in older tourism material are not required parts of this itinerary. Access can change with redevelopment and operations; use public spaces that are open on the day. You can consider the relationship between shops and streets without entering the buildings.\n\nFind a safe place to stop before looking across a busy road. If taking photographs, make one image of a building and another including the street. Together, they help you reconstruct entrances and connections later, rather than preserving only a handsome façade.\n\nOn another visit, change the starting point. If a café organised today’s walk, let a trade you found interesting organise the next. Compare the district’s industry guide with a current map and use public routes. Locations and opening patterns may change: a closed door on one afternoon is not evidence that a business has permanently shut.\n\n![Cheonggyecheon stream in central Seoul](/assets/editorial-2026-09/seoul-cheonggyecheon.jpg \"Cheonggyecheon · Jjw / Wikimedia Commons / CC BY 4.0 · 13 April 2024\")\n\n## Let Cheonggyecheon be an optional finish\n\nFinish in the direction of Cheonggyecheon. If the waterside path is open and comfortable to use, follow an available entrance. If it is closed or the descent is inconvenient, end at street level instead. Choosing a return point that suits the weather and your mobility is part of planning a walk.\n\nThe suggested sequence is **Euljiro 3-ga area → Inhyeon-dong printing lanes → food or coffee → Sewoon Sangga surroundings → towards Cheonggyecheon**. Shorten the later sections if you spend longer somewhere. Readers interested in shops can focus on the lanes and a meal; those interested in buildings can give Sewoon more time. This is not a shortest-route itinerary or a promise of a fixed walking duration.\n\nIf one trade name stays with you on the journey home, the walk has done its work. You may notice it again when looking at a menu or opening a parcel. Euljiro can become a place where everyday objects reconnect with a memory of travel.\n\n## Sources and production\n\nPhotos: [Aaaatu original 1](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_printing_company_in_Euljiro_printing_alley.jpg), [original 2](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_printing_company_in_Euljiro_printing_alley,_printing_operator_working_on_quality_control.jpg), [original 3](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Another_printing_company_in_Euljiro.jpg), and [Jjw original 4](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20240413_Cheonggyecheon.jpg). Licences: photos 1–3 [CC BY-SA 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/); photo 4 [CC BY 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).\n\nRoute: SignedPrice assembled this suggested walk from public sources. It is not a recent field report or a source of current opening hours; check operating information before visiting.",
